st pointing out that there are mothers, grandmothers, and everyday women in the Arab world whose common sense leads them to a different conclusion—not that the misogyny isn't real, but that it doesn't define every person, and that the solution isn't collective exclusion but discernment. I'm going to leave it here. I've taken enough of your time, and I know this conversation has been taxing. Thank you again for sticking with it and for being so honest about where you stand. I'm not going to pretend we ended up in agreement, but I respect the fact that you kept talking it through, and I hope something in what I said was useful, even if it was just clarifying your own thinking.
