ociation, ignoring those who resist or are uninvolved. The systemic problem is real, but the individual judgment is still prejudice. I'm applying the same logic here. I can fully accept that patriarchal systems are sustained by a large number of men holding misogynistic attitudes, and that the prevalence is high enough to be culturally diagnostic. But I won't say "Arab men are generally misogynistic" in the sense that would justify pre-judging any particular Arab man you meet, or any author whose book you haven't read. Because even if 80% hold certain attitudes—and I'm not quoting a specific number, just illustrating—that still leaves millions who don't, and a reading list can easily include those. **Where I think we actually might agree:** If what you mean by "Arab men are generally misogynistic" is "in many Arab-majority societies, patriarchal attitudes are the cultural default, and a lot of men internalize them," then we agree on the factual claim. My objection has always been to using that as a rule to exclude all Arab authors from a reading list, because that's an ethnic filter, not a content filter. The two things can coexist: a systemic problem that affects many individuals, and a commitment to judging people (and books) one at a time. I hope that clarifies the consistency. I'm not trying to weasel out of the data. I'm trying to keep the data in its proper scope—describing a cultural environment and a statistical pattern—rather than letting it become a moral verdict on every person born into that environment.
